  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nAside from some drizzle, precip has tapered off early this morning. \nWestern and central Minnesota were the winners in terms of rainfall \ntotals, with around 0.75\" to 1\" or better for the past 24 hours. Far \nwest central Minnesota did see a handful of amounts greater than \n1.5\" to 2\". This will hopefully be reflected as some beneficial \nrainfall for this week's drought monitor update. Looking at surface \nobs, a surface low is centered just north of St. Cloud, with winds \nturning west to northwesterly across roughly the western half of \nMinnesota. It's apparent where southerly flow is still in control, \nwith both MSP and STC hitting their daily high temperatures just \nbefore midnight. It's likely that today's high's will occur early \nthis morning and will slowly fall or remain steady through this \nafternoon. This surface low will continue to move to the north-\nnortheast as a second surface low moves up out of the Central \nPlains, bringing with it another round of showers by late \nmorning/early afternoon. The trajectory of this second low will be \nfurther east into central Wisconsin, placing western and central \nMinnesota under the axis of heaviest precip. However, this second \nround will come with lower QPF amounts than what we saw Monday. \nStill, additional rainfall amounts of 0.5\" to 1\" are expected \nthrough Thursday. North-northwest winds will remain on the breezy \nside for the western half of the CWA during this time period with \ngusts up to 30 MPH possible.\n\nThis system finally moves out Thursday with temperatures returning \nto more typical November normals (highs in the upper 40s/low 50s, \nlows in the low 30s). While Friday will be dry, it may end up still \nbeing a fairly cloudy day. A shortwave looks to move through \nSaturday, bringing some low PoPs back to the forecast. Following \nthis, temperatures climb back into the mid to upper 50s by the end \nof the period. We'll start next week dry and likely stay that way \nwith high pressure building in through mid week.",
